Notes
Type mismatch: if EmplClass.LabourType and EmployeeLabour.LabourType have different data types, SQL Server will convert implicitly or throw an error. If you get an error, send me both column types.
No match in EmplClass: on create, the insert falls back to model.LabourType. On update, it keeps the existing value.
Hours format: I changed hrsReqd on create to {model.LabourMins:D2} so it stores 2:00, not 2:0. The SQL parsing handles both.
Existing bad rows: the new estimates already saved still have NULLs. Back up the table first and test on a copy, then run this once:
sql
UPDATE EL
SET LabourType   = ISNULL(EL.LabourType, EC.LabourType),
    TimeAddition = CASE WHEN ISNULL(TRY_CAST(REPLACE(CAST(EL.TimeAddition AS VARCHAR(30)), ',', '.') AS DECIMAL(18,6)), 0) = 0
                        THEN ROUND(TRY_CAST(LEFT(EL.HrsReqd, CHARINDEX(':', EL.HrsReqd) - 1) AS DECIMAL(18,6))
                             + TRY_CAST(SUBSTRING(EL.HrsReqd, CHARINDEX(':', EL.HrsReqd) + 1, 10) AS DECIMAL(18,6)) / 60.0, 2)
                        ELSE EL.TimeAddition END
FROM SBCES.EmployeeLabour EL
OUTER APPLY (SELECT TOP 1 LabourType FROM SBCES.EmplClass
             WHERE UPPER(LabourDefn) = UPPER(EL.LabourDefn)) EC
WHERE CHARINDEX(':', EL.HrsReqd) > 0
  AND (EL.LabourType IS NULL OR ISNULL(TRY_CAST(REPLACE(CAST(EL.TimeAddition AS VARCHAR(30)), ',', '.') AS DECIMAL(18,6)), 0) = 0);
SQL fallback: the CostHrs fallback from my last message (use HrsReqd when TimeAddition is empty) is still worth adding to STEP 3 and STEP 4, as a safety net for rows saved by any other path.
Other write paths: any other action that inserts into EmployeeLabour (copy or clone, rebuilt-part propagation, import) needs the same logic, or it will keep producing NULLs.
